ad the evidence" data-passages="forklift run into and hit the pillars">2</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The board's August minutes record a 23-to-3 vote directing closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorizing legal steps to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-13" data-record-href="/records/board-minutes-68-3-2026-08-13/" data-passage="Motion carried by roll call, 23 in favor, 3 opposed" aria-label="Source 13: read the evidence" data-passages="seeking to dissolve the preliminary injunction">13</a></sup></p></section><section id="outlets"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Cross-source check</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong</h2><div data-layer="fact"><article class="outlet-check warn"><h3>Associated Press via PBS NewsHour, September 17, 2026</h3><p class="oc-claim"><span>reported that the order required 30 days' notice before any major physical change, including demolition.</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><strong>Too broad</strong><span>.</span></p><p class="oc-record"><span>The reproduced order says more than 30 days and ties the requirement to changes in the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court.</span></p><p class="oc-sources"><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-20" data-record-href="/records/pbs-ap-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="provide 30 days' notice before making any major physical changes" aria-label="Source 20: read the evidence">20</a></sup></p></article><article class="outlet-check ok"><h3>UPI, September 17, 2026</h3><p class="oc-claim"><span>reproduced the operative sentence tying advance notice to project-scope changes, including demolition.</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><strong>Checks out</strong><span> against the same language reproduced by the parties' counsel and the surrounding filings.</span></p><p class="oc-sources"><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a></sup></p></article><article class="outlet-check ok"><h3>USA Today, September 17, 2026</h3><p class="oc-claim"><span>reported that the order blocked immediate demolition rather than deciding whether demolition could ever occur.</span></p><p class="oc-verdict"><strong>Checks out</strong><span>.</span></p><p class="oc-record"><span>The mechanism is advance notice; the order did not decide the legality of a future demolition plan.</span></p><p class="oc-sources"><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-23" data-record-href="/records/usa-today-kennedy-center-order-2026-09-17/" data-passage="blocked President Donald Trump from taking immediate action to demolish the Kennedy Center" aria-label="Source 23: read the evidence">23</a></sup></p></article></div></section><section id="claim-check"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The headline claim</p><h2 data-layer="fact">Claim checked against the record</h2><div data-layer="fact"><div class="contested-block"><p class="cb-label">The judge said Trump must give 30 days&#39; notice before demolishing the Kennedy Center</p><p>Directionally right, but incomplete. The order binds the defendants and requires written notice to the plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before implementing a change to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court, including demolition. It does not identify a demolition plan, decide whether one would be lawful or impose a general notice rule for every physical change.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a></sup></p></div></div></section><section id="what-happened-next"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">The next dated steps</p><h2 data-layer="fact">The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks</h2><p data-layer="narrative">The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Requests for Written and Document Discovery" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence" data-passages="All Discovery Closed">15</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-18" data-record-href="/records/beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17/" data-passage="required the Board to update the Court by September 23" aria-label="Source 18: read the evidence">18</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-21" data-record-href="/records/upi-kennedy-center-2026-09-17/" data-passage="more than 30 days before the implementation" aria-label="Source 21: read the evidence">21</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-15" data-record-href="/records/scheduling-order-82-2026-09-16/" data-passage="Motion to Vacate the Preliminary" aria-label="Source 15: read the evidence">15</a></sup></p><p data-layer="narrative">On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.<sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-24" data-record-href="/records/defendants-motion-vacate-88-2026-09-18/" data-passage="DEFENDANTS’ MOTION TO DISSOLVE PRELIMINARY INJUNCTION" aria-label="Source 24: read the evidence">24</a></sup><sup class="src-ref"><a href="#src-25" data-record-href="/records/defendants-summary-judgment-89-2026-09-18/" data-passage="DEFENDANTS’ RENEWED MOTION FOR" aria-label="Source 25: read the evidence">25</a></sup></p></section><section id="unknowns"><p class="sec-kicker" data-layer="narrative">Open questions</p><h2 data-layer="fact">What we do not know yet</h2><ul data-layer="fact"><li data-absence>Whether the September 23 status report was filed, and whether the initial safety closure was extended. Searched: the admitted docket material and coverage through September 19; the report was not yet due.</li><li data-absence>Whether the defendants have developed a project-scope change that would trigger the notice order. Searched: the admitted board records, plans, filings and coverage; none contains an identified demolition plan.</li><li data-absence>The court's minute order as a standalone, court-hosted document. Searched: the District Court docket, CourtListener and Justia; the docket routes were unavailable to the capture registry and the Justia mirror stopped before September 17. The page therefore labels the carriers of the quoted sentence.</li></ul></section><section id="records" data-layer="fact"><p class="sec-kicker">Sources</p><h2>The records</h2><p class="section-lede">Everything this page rests on, numbered where the story cites it. We keep a dated copy of each one.</p><h3 class="src-group">Primary documents</h3><ol class="src-list" start="1"><li id="src-1" value="1"><a href="/records/beatty-motion-84-2026-09-16/">Plaintiff motion for an emergency hearing</a> · U.S. District Court for the District of Columbia, 2026-09-16 <span class="src-usage" data-layer="proof"> &middot; Beatty’s request for an emergency hearing over the closure</span></li>