   115	What changes in practice
   116	The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure
   117	The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope change. It adds written notice to the earlier pledge to report a reversal of the no-demolition position.
   118	21
   119	7
   120	The defendants called the September 16 safety closure separate from the renovation decision. Beatty's reply disputed that account and argued that the temporary explanation was a pretext for an indefinite closure. Cooper denied the requested hearing.
   121	4
   122	6
   123	21
   124	Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling plaster after water damage. The same declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels and said one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.
   125	5
   126	Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion, said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit exterior pillars several times. That observation was sworn testimony from one witness, not a finding by the court.
   127	2
   128	The board's August minutes record a 23-to-3 vote directing closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorizing legal steps to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.
   129	13
   130	Cross-source check
   131	What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong
   132	Associated Press via PBS NewsHour, September 17, 2026
   133	reported that the order required 30 days' notice before any major physical change, including demolition.
   134	Too broad
   135	.
   136	The reproduced order says more than 30 days and ties the requirement to changes in the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court.
   137	20
   138	UPI, September 17, 2026
   139	reproduced the operative sentence tying advance notice to project-scope changes, including demolition.
   140	Checks out
   141	against the same language reproduced by the parties' counsel and the surrounding filings.
   142	21
   143	USA Today, September 17, 2026
   144	reported that the order blocked immediate demolition rather than deciding whether demolition could ever occur.
   145	Checks out
   146	.
   147	The mechanism is advance notice; the order did not decide the legality of a future demolition plan.
   148	23
   149	The headline claim
   150	Claim checked against the record
   151	The judge said Trump must give 30 days' notice before demolishing the Kennedy Center
   152	Directionally right, but incomplete. The order binds the defendants and requires written notice to the plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before implementing a change to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court, including demolition. It does not identify a demolition plan, decide whether one would be lawful or impose a general notice rule for every physical change.
   153	21
   154	The next dated steps
   155	The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks
   156	The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.
   157	15
   158	The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.
   159	18
   160	21
   161	15
   162	On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.
   163	24
   164	25
