data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/cbs-kennedy-center-2026-09-17.html:1105:    <p>A federal judge on Thursday ordered the Trump administration to give 30 days' notice before any "demolition" occurs at the Kennedy Center, an order that came less than 24 hours after President Trump said that the performing arts center could be "ripped down," and photos showed him looking at a document that said "Kennedy Center Demolished."&nbsp;</p><p>Before Mr. Trump's comments, a spokesperson for the Kennedy Center insisted Wednesday that "nothing is being demolished" at the performing arts center.</p><p>U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper on Thursday issued an order requiring a detailed report from the Justice Department by next Wednesday on the current "temporary closure" of the center and emergency repairs described in court filings by the center's executive director, Matt Floca.</p><p>The Justice Department said in court documents Thursday that the duration of the closure will be for one week, "unless extended" to address safety hazards.&nbsp;</p><p>In a&nbsp;<a href="https://storage.courtlistener.com/recap/gov.uscourts.dcd.287972/gov.uscourts.dcd.287972.86.1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">declaration</a>&nbsp;submitted by the Justice Department prior to Cooper's ruling, Floca said that "acute risks to public safety resulting from continued structural deterioration ... necessitated a temporary closure of most public spaces in the main building." In an&nbsp;<a href="https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/72069932/86/2/beatty-v-trump/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">email</a>&nbsp;to Kennedy Center staff that was also submitted to the court, Floca said that any extensions of the closure would be evaluated on a week-to-week basis.</p><p>"To be clear, this is not a decision to effectuate the Board's separate vote to close the building for renovation," Floca wrote in the email. "It is a distinct decision by management to address imminent life-safety risks pending the Court's ruling on that vote."</p><p>Cooper on Thursday also denied a request from Rep. Joyce Beatty, an ex officio board member who sued last year to halt Mr. Trump's changes to the Kennedy Center, for an emergency hearing on what she called the "unlawful closure of the Kennedy Center."</p>
