98:      {"@context":"https:\/\/schema.org","@type":"NewsArticle","articleBody":"A federal judge on Thursday ordered the Trump administration to give 30 days\u0027 notice before any \"demolition\" occurs at the Kennedy Center, an order that came less than 24 hours after President Trump said that the performing arts center could be \"ripped down,\" and photos showed him looking at a document that said \"Kennedy Center Demolished.\"\u00a0Before Mr. Trump\u0027s comments, a spokesperson for the Kennedy Center insisted Wednesday that \"nothing is being demolished\" at the performing arts center.U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper on Thursday issued an order requiring a detailed report from the Justice Department by next Wednesday on the current \"temporary closure\" of the center and emergency repairs described in court filings by the center\u0027s executive director, Matt Floca.The Justice Department said in court documents Thursday that the duration of the closure will be for one week, \"unless extended\" to address safety hazards.\u00a0In a\u00a0declaration\u00a0submitted by the Justice Department prior to Cooper\u0027s ruling, Floca said that \"acute risks to public safety resulting from continued structural deterioration ... necessitated a temporary closure of most public spaces in the main building.\" In an\u00a0email\u00a0to Kennedy Center staff that was also submitted to the court, Floca said that any extensions of the closure would be evaluated on a week-to-week basis.\"To be clear, this is not a decision to effectuate the Board\u0027s separate vote to close the building for renovation,\" Floca wrote in the email. \"It is a distinct decision by management to address imminent life-safety risks pending the Court\u0027s ruling on that vote.\"Cooper on Thursday also denied a request from Rep. Joyce Beatty, an ex officio board member who sued last year to halt Mr. Trump\u0027s changes to the Kennedy Center, for an emergency hearing on what she called the \"unlawful closure of the Kennedy Center.\"Beatty, in court documents filed Wednesday, argued, \"Given the unexplained and unlawful closure, it is possible Defendants intend to undertake immediate steps in order to make public access practically impossible for the foreseeable future, including by demolishing public avenues of access to the building, and thereby evade the Court\u0027s order by fait accompli.\"Beatty also noted in the filing that a witness said they saw a \"forklift run into and hit the pillars on the building several times.\"Fencing was also erected around the Kennedy Center on Wednesday.Roma Davari, the Kennedy Center\u0027s vice president of public relations, told \"Real America\u0027s Voice\" Wednesday night that \"nothing is being demolished\" and that people should ignore \"the media that is saying that because no one has ever said that coming from the institution.\"Thursday\u0027s ruling marks the latest in the ongoing fight over Mr. Trump\u0027s vision for renovations to the performing arts center.Cooper ruled Tuesday that Mr. Trump\u0027s name cannot be put\u00a0on the building, following a second attempt by the center\u0027s board to add it, saying: \"Defendants cannot install memorials for President Trump or anyone or anything else at the Kennedy Center without Congress\u0027s blessing. The board resolution bucks a federal court order and a statute Congress enacted.\"  Shortly afterward, the board, which is filled with allies of Mr. Trump\u0027s, voted to close the center for renovations, a source said. Congress last year appropriated more than $250 million for the restoration. Cooper, who has been overseeing the case, must approve the board\u0027s decision, but he has not yet determined whether the vote was lawful.Mr. Trump on Wednesday night reiterated that if he were to raise money for the Kennedy Center\u0027s restoration, his administration should be recognized \u2014 or the site\u0027s future would be in question.\"So for the Trump administration, for me, to get involved and to take that on long term and to carry it, or to raise money to carry it, I think that the Trump administration should certainly have recognition,\" Mr. Trump said. \"Because frankly, if we don\u0027t do that, it\u0027s going to close. It\u0027ll end up being ripped down.\"On Air Force One Wednesday night, Mr. Trump could be seen looking at a document that said \"Kennedy Center Demolished\" as he was returning from a rally in North Carolina. The photo was entered as evidence in the emergency motion.The Kennedy Center, which opened in 1971, is a national cultural center and is the official \"living memorial\" to President John F. Kennedy.","dateModified":"2026-09-17T17:51:56-0400","articleSection":["Politics"],"author":[{"@type":"Person","name":"Jacob  Rosen","url":"https:\/\/www.cbsnews.com\/team\/jacob-rosen\/","description":"Jake Rosen is a reporter covering the Department of Justice. He was previously a campaign digital reporter covering President Trump\u0027s 2024 campaign and also served as an associate producer for &quot;Face the Nation with Margaret Brennan,&quot; where he worked with Brennan for two years on the broadcast. \r\n\r\nRosen has been a producer for several CBS News podcasts, including &quot;The Takeout,&quot; &quot;The Debrief&quot; and &quot;Agent of Betrayal: The Double Life of Robert Hanssen.&quot;\r\n\r\nIn 2022, Rosen was part of the team that won a Walter Cronkite Award for Excellence in TV Political Journalism for his work with &quot;Face the Nation,&quot; covering the aftermath of the Jan. 6, 2021, riot at the U.S. Capitol and attacks on democracy.\r\n\r\nDuring the COVID-19 pandemic and the 2020 election, Rosen assisted in covering the White House for CBS News Radio under both the Trump and Biden administrations. He graduated from the George Washington University with a degree in journalism."},{"@type":"Person","name":"Aaron  Navarro","url":"https:\/\/www.cbsnews.com\/team\/aaron-navarro\/","description":"Aaron Navarro is a CBS News digital reporter who covered the 2024 elections. Since joining the political unit in 2019, Aaron has covered redistricting, Virginia Gov. Glenn Youngkin\u0027s 2021 campaign, the 2020 and 2022 congressional races, and the major political parties. After graduating from the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign, he worked as a news associate for &quot;Face the Nation with Margaret Brennan&quot; and the &quot;CBS Evening News.&quot; He\u0027s previously written for Fake Shore Drive, a blog covering Chicago\u0027s rap scene."},{"@type":"Person","name":"Caroline  Linton","url":"https:\/\/www.cbsnews.com\/team\/caroline-linton\/","description":"Caroline Linton is an associate managing editor on the political team for CBSNews.com. A graduate of Craig Newmark Graduate School of Journalism at CUNY, Caroline has filed stories from locations ranging from outside kindergarten on the first day of school to the headquarters of the European Union in Brussels, and has worked every shift from pre-dawn to overnight.\r\n\r\nPrior to joining CBS News, she worked for The Daily Beast, Newsweek, WNYC and amNewYork, among other digital and print outlets, and an article she wrote once inspired a stranger to donate a kidney. Caroline currently writes and edits about local, state and national politics."}],"mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"NewsArticle","@id":"https:\/\/www.cbsnews.com\/news\/kennedy-center-demolished-trump-photos\/","inLanguage  ":"en","name":"Kennedy Center says \"nothing is being demolished\" as Trump says it could be \"ripped down\"","headline":"Kennedy Center says \"nothing is being demolished\" as Trump says it could be \"ripped down\"","description":"In court documents filed Wednesday by Rep. Joyce Beatty, a witness said he saw a \"forklift run into and hit the pillars\" of the building.","isAccessibleForFree":true},"headline":"Kennedy Center says \"nothing is being demolished\" as Trump says it could be \"ripped down\"","description":"In court documents filed Wednesday by Rep. Joyce Beatty, a witness said he saw a \"forklift run into and hit the pillars\" of the building.","url":"https:\/\/www.cbsnews.com\/news\/kennedy-center-demolished-trump-photos\/","image":{"@type":"ImageObject","height":630,"width":1200,"url":"https:\/\/assets2.cbsnewsstatic.com\/hub\/i\/r\/2026\/09\/17\/c4c52ce9-552d-4181-84fa-6e36cc154d4d\/thumbnail\/1200x630\/20392d705a6b5411a228104f59b76086\/gettyimages-2295131860.jpg"},"thumbnailUrl":"https:\/\/assets2.cbsnewsstatic.com\/hub\/i\/r\/2026\/09\/17\/c4c52ce9-552d-4181-84fa-6e36cc154d4d\/thumbnail\/1200x630\/20392d705a6b5411a228104f59b76086\/gettyimages-2295131860.jpg","keywords":["Kennedy Center","Donald Trump"],"datePublished":"2026-09-17T11:06:00-0400","publisher":{"@type":"NewsMediaOrganization","@id":"https:\/\/www.cbsnews.com\/","name":"CBS News"},"alternativeHeadline":"Trump photo shows him viewing \"Kennedy Center demolished\" document"}
