    55	   demolished.^[31]3^[32]26
    56	
    57	   Cooper denied the hearing. He nevertheless ordered the defendants to give plaintiffs in both cases written notice before implementing a covered change.^[33]21
    58	   U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper�September 17, 2026�Minute order, as reproduced by UPI
    59	
    60	     To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to provide such notice in writing to
    61	     Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League
    62	     [a separate case], including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building.^[34]21
    63	
    64	   The order also required the administration to update Cooper by September 23 on the status of the temporary closure and emergency repairs. Beatty's counsel said Cooper also
    65	   ordered discovery into the board's actions related to a potential demolition.^[35]19^[36]18
    66	
    67	   The catch
    68	    1. The period is more than 30 days.The operative sentence says notice must arrive more than 30 days before implementation. "30 days" is a useful shorthand, but it drops
    69	       the word that sets the boundary.^[37]21
    70	    2. The order follows the plans already put before the court.It does not say "any major physical changes" require notice. That phrase comes from the AP headline. The
    71	       trigger in the reproduced order is a change to the scope of the project plans presented in the preservation case, with demolition named as an example.^[38]20^[39]21
    72	    3. Notice is not a final ruling on demolition.The reproduced sentence imposes an advance-notice requirement. It neither approves nor permanently bars demolition.^[40]21
    73	
    74	   The earlier record
    75	
    76	The court had relied on a no-demolition position
    77	
    78	   In May, Cooper denied preliminary relief in the preservation case after considering sworn statements about the project. The opinion said the building's footprint and
    79	   overall shape and size seemed unlikely to change permanently, and it described the anticipated open-space changes as relatively limited. The court said those findings were
    80	   tentative and could change if the facts changed.^[41]9
    81	
    82	   The parties' August 28 joint report made the relevant promise explicit: the defendants would inform the plaintiffs and the court if the board's plans reversed the position
    83	   that the building would not be demolished and no new structure would be built.^[42]7
    84	   How the notice order aroseThe dated sequence in the court and board records.
    85	   DateDecision
    86	   May 29The court denies preliminary relief in the preservation case. The ruling relies on sworn representations that the main building would not be demolished and no new
    87	   structure would be built.
    88	   Aug. 13The board votes 23 to 3 to close the main building for renovation. Programming would continue at the REACH and off site.
    89	   Aug. 28The parties file a joint status report. The defendants again say the building will not be demolished and no new structure will be constructed.
    90	   Sept. 15The board votes again to close the main building. Its chairman says renovation is to wait while an appeal proceeds.
    91	   Sept. 16The main building closes temporarily. The executive director says the initial safety closure will last seven days unless extended.
    92	   Sept. 17The judge denies an emergency hearing and orders advance notice. Covered project-scope changes require written notice more than 30 days before implementation.
    93	   Sept. 23Status report due. The court requires a sworn declaration addressing the temporary closure and emergency repairs.
    94	
    95	   What changes in practice
    96	
    97	The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure
    98	
    99	   The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope change. It adds written notice to the
   100	   earlier pledge to report a reversal of the no-demolition position.^[43]21^[44]7
   101	
   102	   The defendants called the September 16 safety closure separate from the renovation decision. Beatty's reply disputed that account and argued that the temporary explanation
   103	   was a pretext for an indefinite closure. Cooper denied the requested hearing.^[45]4^[46]6^[47]21
   104	
   105	   Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of a five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling
   106	   plaster after water damage. The same declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels and said one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.^[48]5
   107	
   108	   Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion, said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit exterior pillars several times. That
   109	   observation was sworn testimony from one witness, not a finding by the court.^[49]2
   110	
   111	   The board's August minutes record a 23-to-3 vote directing closure of the main building for restoration and renovation and authorizing legal steps to dissolve the
   112	   injunction that prevented that closure.^[50]13
   113	
   114	   Cross-source check
   115	
   116	What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong
   117	
   118	Associated Press via PBS NewsHour, September 17, 2026
   119	
   120	   reported that the order required 30 days' notice before any major physical change, including demolition.
   121	
   122	   Too broad.
   123	
   124	   The reproduced order says more than 30 days and ties the requirement to changes in the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court.
   125	
   126	   ^[51]20
   127	
   128	UPI, September 17, 2026
   129	
   130	   reproduced the operative sentence tying advance notice to project-scope changes, including demolition.
   131	
   132	   Checks out against the same language reproduced by the parties' counsel and the surrounding filings.
   133	
   134	   ^[52]21
   135	
   136	USA Today, September 17, 2026
   137	
   138	   reported that the order blocked immediate demolition rather than deciding whether demolition could ever occur.
   139	
   140	   Checks out.
   141	
   142	   The mechanism is advance notice; the order did not decide the legality of a future demolition plan.
   143	
   144	   ^[53]23
   145	
   146	   The headline claim
   147	
   148	Claim checked against the record
   149	
   150	   The judge said Trump must give 30 days' notice before demolishing the Kennedy Center
   151	
   152	   Directionally right, but incomplete. The order binds the defendants and requires written notice to the plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before implementing a
   153	   change to the scope of the project plans previously presented to the court, including demolition. It does not identify a demolition plan, decide whether one would be
   154	   lawful or impose a general notice rule for every physical change.^[54]21
   155	
   156	   The next dated steps
   157	
   158	The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks
   159	
   160	   The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written discovery requests and deposition
   161	   notices, September 30 for written responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the close of discovery.^[55]15
   162	
   163	   The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary
   164	   injunction and for discovery on the closure claim.^[56]18^[57]21^[58]15
   165	
   166	   On September 18, the defendants filed a motion to dissolve the May preliminary injunction and a renewed motion for partial summary judgment on Beatty's remaining closure
   167	   and construction claims. Those motions asked the court for relief; they did not themselves dissolve the injunction or decide the claims.^[59]24^[60]25
   168	
   169	   Open questions
   170	
   171	What we do not know yet
   172	
   173	     * Whether the September 23 status report was filed, and whether the initial safety closure was extended. Searched: the admitted docket material and coverage through
   174	       September 19; the report was not yet due.
   175	     * Whether the defendants have developed a project-scope change that would trigger the notice order. Searched: the admitted board records, plans, filings and coverage;
   176	       none contains an identified demolition plan.
   177	     * The court's minute order as a standalone, court-hosted document. Searched: the District Court docket, CourtListener and Justia; the docket routes were unavailable to
   178	       the capture registry and the Justia mirror stopped before September 17. The page therefore labels the carriers of the quoted sentence.
