           This Court previously found the “plaques in the nature of memorials” clause to be
evidence in support of a broad statutory sweep. Doc. 50 at 52. But “in the nature of” simply means
“of the type, form, or character of; similar to, like; equivalent to, classifiable as.” Of/In the Nature
of, def. 4a, Oxford English Dictionary (rev. ed 2003). Therefore, the statute prohibits only plaques
“classifiable as” memorials, and no more.

