one has moved for a stay which would change the dynamic. This resolution provides
that we want to continue to acknowledge the President’s support and that we want
to abide by the court order. Someone could move for a stay, and DOJ can then move
for a stay. The Center could appeal.

Representative Larsen thanked Mr. Berke for the discussion. He did not think that
what Secretary Lutnick outlined was exclusive. He understands from the President
that he would have preferred a stay. But it would be a solution to one of the issues.
Representative Larsen then yielded back his time to the chair.

Mr. Manchester restated his resolution to stay the order. It was seconded by Brian
Ballard, Trustee.

Maria Bartiromo, Trustee, stated that all the info the Board has has been based on
merit. We cannot be on defense here. We are talking about the Commander in Chief
having a special skill set of building across the world. All the decisions that have been
made have been made on merit.

Ms. Levine, stated that the hesitancy may be due to the fact that what we are
considering is not contradictory to the court’s mandate, we are asking to work with
the court to come up with a solution. We are not in an adversarial position. We are
trying to come up with a solution.



                                                                             Page 8 of 11
      Case 1:25-cv-04480-CRC       Document 68-2      Filed 08/24/26    Page 10 of 12

CONFIDENTIAL
DO NOT DISTRIBUTE                                                              Rev. 8-14-26

Senator Whitehouse noted that the actual operative language of the resolution
neither creates nor approves a fund, which Counsel confirmed. The resolution says
we will abide by the law and by court orders.


V.      Action on Secretary Lutnick’s Resolution

Mr. Falconetti called for a vote on Secretary Lutnick’s resolution. The motion was
passed unanimously.

VI.     Action on Mr. Manchester’s Resolution

Mr. Manchester restated his motion to stay and appeal the court’s orders related to The
Donald J. Trump and the John F. Kennedy Memorial Center for the Performing Arts. Mr. Ballard
seconded it.

Secretary Lutnick noted that it is easy to add a stay. The Justice Department is going
to file documents. Mr. Ballard noted that he thinks it is important to appeal the
judge’s decision. To not appeal makes it look like we did something wrong. Mr. Berke
noted to amend the motion to stay and appeal.

Senator Whitehouse stated that it seems like the Board is going forward with minimal
discussion and with no information regarding a stay and appeal and nothing from
Department of Justice. This does not answer the judge’s question of due diligence
before making these decisions. We should have an adequate presentation from the
litigating lawyers, especially if they think that would not be the best use of trust
assets.

Mr. Berke noted that while the DOJ is the defending counsel, he can speak to the
question. The judge’s decision brings up points we would dispute, for example, the
memorial to President Trump or a plaque in the nature of a memorial. Mr. Berke thinks
there are certainly issues that the judge raised in his opinion that are subject to
appeal.

Representative Beatty stated that she cannot vote or participate in this, however she
reminded the Board members that they are defendants and as someone pointed out,
they have not been adequately briefed. Even if the Center appeals and stays, there is
still a second order. You have an obligation to keep the Center open. There is more
board work to do. The Board members are all named because of their oversight and
responsibility.
                                                                               Page 9 of 11
    Case 1:25-cv-04480-CRC       Document 68-2      Filed 08/24/26    Page 11 of 12

CONFIDENTIAL
DO NOT DISTRIBUTE                                                           Rev. 8-14-26


Secretary Lutnick stated we are going to keep the center open. We are going to have
another board meeting in a couple of weeks and will have full info regarding the
construction. The Board will review it together. The information will be sent to all.

Mr. Manchester stated that we need to stay the order or else it has to be taken down.

Ms. Beatty stated that people need to think carefully. We are approving something
people have not even read. It looked like we were off to a good start with acting like
board members instead of personally or emotionally.

Ms. Levine asked how do we best serve this president? We are all presidential
appointees – how do we best serve him in a loyal and efficient way. We need to do
our due diligence on the second resolution – need to really get to know the issues and
be briefed by the attorneys regarding optics, liabilities, legalities.

Secretary Lutnick stated that the appeal would be ordinary: just asking the judge to
consider a different outcome. There is one judge who is politically motivated against
the President. His wife is politically motivated. The resolution says that we will do
things properly, and we will do it the right way and work through the courts. This is
logical, practical and easy.

Joe LaFauci, VP Board Relations and Government Affair for the Center, read the
Bylaws, Article VII Meetings of the Board – Section 6: Voting. Mr. Falconetti requested
advice of counsel.

Mr. Berke stated that Secretary Lutnick laid it out clearly. The resolution that passed
said that we would lay it out within the law. Requesting an appeal and a stay does not
compromise fiduciary duties. No action will be taken in violation of the law. If there is
a motion to stay and the judge denies it, the letter would have to come down.

Pam Bondi, Trustee, stated that she would have to abstain because the Board is
asking the Department of Justice to do something.

A motion was made to take a roll call vote. The motion was passed by a majority of
Trustees present.

A motion was made on Mr. Manchester’s resolution for a stay and appeal. A roll call
vote was taken and the motion was passed:
       In favor: 15

