     1	Rep. Beatty and Counsel Applaud Judge Cooper’s Ruling Blocking Kennedy Center Demolition | Washington Litigation Group
     2	
     3	WASHINGTON, D.C., September 17, 2026 — Today, Judge Christopher R. Cooper issued a ruling in the ongoing Kennedy Center case that effectively prevents Donald Trump from demolishing the Kennedy Center without notice. Under Judge Cooper’s order, should the Board undertake “any changes to the scope of the project plans […] including but not limited to any ‘demolition’ of the Center’s main building,” they must provide 30 days’ advance warning. Judge Cooper additionally ordered discovery into the Board’s actions related to a potential demolition and required the Board to update the Court by September 23 regarding the latest efforts to shutter the Center.
     4	Following this ruling in Congresswoman Joyce Beatty’s (D-OH-03) favor, she and her counsel issued the following statements:
     5	“It is simply outrageous that we have gotten to the point of consistent judicial intervention to protect this historic institution from getting bulldozed by the President,” said Rep. Beatty. “I will continue to fight for the rule of law and defend this memorial on behalf of the American people.”
     6	“We are grateful Judge Cooper is taking these escalating threats to the Kennedy Center seriously,” said Rep. Beatty’s counsel, Amb. Norm Eisen (ret.) of Democracy Defenders Action and Nathaniel Zelinsky of Washington Litigation Group. “Further defiance by the Trump administration would precipitate this into a full-blown constitutional crisis of the president’s own making. We are already on the precipice. Fortunately, the court and the nation are watching.”
     7	Yesterday, Rep. Beatty filed an emergency motion before a federal district court to enforce compliance with the court’s order following the Kennedy Center’s unlawful closure. The building is currently inaccessible to the public during normal operating hours today, despite a federal court order barring Donald Trump and his handpicked board from shutting down the Kennedy Center. Under Judge Cooper’s order, Defendants must provide information regarding the closure by September 23.
     8	Rep. Beatty, an ex officio trustee of the Kennedy Center, is represented by Nathaniel Zelinsky, Kyle Freeny, and Alex Kristofcak of Washington Litigation Group, and Norm Eisen, Steve Jonas, David W. Ogden, and Joshua Kolb of Democracy Defenders Action.
     9	To request an interview or for questions, please contact Teresa Mozur with Democracy Defenders Action at [email protected] and Xochitl Hinojosa and Josh Schwerin for Washington Litigation Group at [email protected].
    10	# # #
    11	Democracy Defenders Action brings together a nonpartisan team to work with national, state, and local allies across the country to defend in real time the foundations of our democracy.
    12	Washington Litigation Group is a nonpartisan team of attorneys dedicated to defending individuals and institutions that have been unlawfully targeted for exercising their legal rights and to fighting against governmental overreach.     1	Judge: Kennedy Center must submit plans for changes 30 days out - UPI.com
     2	
     3	Sept. 17 (UPI) -- A judge ordered Thursday that the Trump administration must submit any plans to change the John F. Kennedy Center for the Performing Arts in writing 30 days in advance of the work.
     4	The decision came in response to Rep. Joyce Beatty, D-Ohio, an ex-officio Kennedy Center board member, who had requested a hearing after President Donald Trump and the board shut down the center Tuesday.
     5	Kennedy Center Executive Director Matt Floca responded that the closure was only for seven days "unless extended" to address "acute risks to public safety" and was "separate from any Board decision related to closure for ongoing renovations."
     6	This case is part of an ongoing lawsuit filed by Beatty to keep Trump's name off of the building.
     7	The president took over the Kennedy Center soon after he took office in 2025, filling the board with loyalists. They announced the center was closing Tuesday after a judge ruled for a second time that he couldn't put his name on the building.
     8	Judge Christopher Cooper also ruled against a plan to add an inscription below the sign that says "renovated and restored by Donald J. Trump."
     9	Cooper wrote in his Thursday decision that Beatty's request for a hearing was denied.
    10	But he added another order.
    11	"To avoid any confusion on that score given more recent events, the Court hereby ORDERS Defendants [the Kennedy Center board] to provide such notice in writing to Plaintiffs in both cases more than 30 days before the implementation of any changes to the scope of the project plans presented to the Court in D.C. Preservation League [a separate case], including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building," the ruling said.
    12	Trump has threatened to demolish the building if he doesn't get to put his name on it, saying that it's in poor condition.
    13	"The closing will take place immediately, however, the Renovation and Reconstruction, which is a very large and complex job, cannot begin until such time as the D.C. Circuit rules on the Board's approved name," he posted Tuesday on Truth Social. "If the ruling is a negative one, which it should not be, and is not overturned by the U.S. Supreme Court, the Reconstruction and the Renovation of the Kennedy Center will not take place."
    14	On Wednesday night, a photo was taken through the window of Air Force One of Trump holding a poster that appeared to say, "Kennedy Center DEMOLISH." The image has circulated around social media causing alarm.
    15	"Closing the Kennedy Center without Judge Cooper's approval appears to directly violate his order. This is an assault on the rule of law," said Norm Eisen, one of Beatty's lawyers.
    16	"The board cannot simply ignore Judge Cooper's rulings whenever it suits them. In this country, no one -- including the president -- is above the law," he added.     1	Judge says Trump has to give 30 days notice before demolishing Kennedy Center - ABC News
     2	
     3	Trump was seen with a placard that appeared to say, "Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED."
     4	A federal judge on Thursday directed the Trump administration to provide 30 days' notice of any changes in the Kennedy Center's renovation plans, "including but not limited to any 'demolition' of the Center's main building," as the jockeying over the future of the performing arts center continued.
     5	U.S. District Judge Christopher Cooper made his ruling the afternoon after President Donald Trump was photographed looking over a placard that appeared to include the words "Kennedy Center DEMOLISHED" over what appeared to be an image of a demolition.
     6	In a federal court filing Thursday morning, Kennedy Center executive director Matt Floca said the current closure of the main building is only "temporary," set to last for seven days, and will be reevaluated on a weekly basis.
     7	Judge Cooper, in his ruling, ordered the administration to update him on the status of the "'temporary closure' and emergency repairs" by next Wednesday.
     8	The ruling came as Cooper turned down a request from Ohio Democratic Rep. Joyce Beatty for an emergency hearing in the case, citing the morning's announcement.
     9	The photos of Trump examining the placard were taken Wednesday night through a cabin window on Air Force One by Agence France-Presse photojournalist Brendan Smialowski, who was acting as the pool photographer for White House coverage, as Trump landed at Joint Base Andrews.
    10	It was not immediately clear what the full contents of the placard were or how they were generated. The published images don't show the entire placard and the word that appears to be "DEMOLISHED" was not entirely visible, cut off as "DEMOLIS."
    11	Asked for comment, White House spokesperson Liz Huston said, "For years, Democrats let the Kennedy Center rot, allowing water damage and crumbling infrastructure to turn a national treasure into a building in decay. As President Trump said last night, without his intervention, The Kennedy Center will inevitably continue to decline."
    12	The images were published on Wednesday evening, shortly after the president said the performing arts center could "close" or be "ripped down" if his administration doesn't receive recognition on the performing arts site.
    13	"I think that the Trump administration should certainly have recognition. Because frankly, if we don't do that, it's going to close. It'll end up being ripped down," the president told reporters on the tarmac in North Carolina.
    14	Congress last year appropriated $257 million for needed renovations. But Trump said in a social media post on Tuesday that those renovations will not take place unless his name is again added onto the building.
    15	His name had been added to the center's exterior after the Trump-controlled board voted in December to rename the building the "Trump-Kennedy Center."
    16	Judge Cooper ruled in May that Trump's name had to come down. Cooper wrote in a decision that the center's rebranding violated the law. He ordered that Trump's name be removed from the building within two weeks.

