   What changes in practice

The plaintiffs get warning; the parties dispute the immediate safety closure

   The reproduced order gives the plaintiffs in both cases a warning
   period before the defendants implement a covered project-scope change.
   It adds written notice to the earlier pledge to report a reversal of
   the no-demolition position.^21^7

   The defendants called the September 16 safety closure separate from the
   renovation decision. Beatty's reply disputed that account and argued
   that the temporary explanation was a pretext for an indefinite closure.
   Cooper denied the requested hearing.^4^6^21

   Floca identified two immediate safety grounds: an engineering
   assessment of the roof-terrace canopy and the September 4 failure of a
   five-by-four-foot piece of ceiling plaster after water damage. The same
   declaration reported severe corrosion in 46 of 278 panels and said
   one-third of the panels required immediate replacement.^5

   Bridget Kennedy-Bailey, whose declaration supported Beatty's motion,
   said she saw fencing block the building and saw a forklift hit exterior
   pillars several times. That observation was sworn testimony from one
   witness, not a finding by the court.^2

   The board's August minutes record a 23-to-3 vote directing closure of
   the main building for restoration and renovation and authorizing legal
   steps to dissolve the injunction that prevented that closure.^13

   Cross-source check

What the coverage got right, and what it got wrong

Associated Press via PBS NewsHour, September 17, 2026

   reported that the order required 30 days' notice before any major
   physical change, including demolition.

   Too broad.

   The reproduced order says more than 30 days and ties the requirement to
   changes in the scope of the project plans previously presented to the
   court.

   ^20

UPI, September 17, 2026

   reproduced the operative sentence tying advance notice to project-scope
   changes, including demolition.

   Checks out against the same language reproduced by the parties' counsel
   and the surrounding filings.

   ^21

USA Today, September 17, 2026

   reported that the order blocked immediate demolition rather than
   deciding whether demolition could ever occur.

   Checks out.

   The mechanism is advance notice; the order did not decide the legality
   of a future demolition plan.

   ^23

   The headline claim

Claim checked against the record

   The judge said Trump must give 30 days' notice before demolishing the
   Kennedy Center

   Directionally right, but incomplete. The order binds the defendants and
   requires written notice to the plaintiffs in both cases more than 30
   days before implementing a change to the scope of the project plans
   previously presented to the court, including demolition. It does not
   identify a demolition plan, decide whether one would be lawful or
   impose a general notice rule for every physical change.^21

   The next dated steps

The closure and renovation claims moved onto separate tracks

   The existing scheduling order had already opened expedited discovery on
   Beatty's closure claim. It set September 23 deadlines for written
   discovery requests and deposition notices, September 30 for written
   responses, October 7 for completed depositions and October 9 for the
   close of discovery.^15

   The September 17 order added the update and advance-notice
   requirements. The September 16 scheduling order separately set
   deadlines for a motion to vacate the preliminary injunction and for
