The X video was reviewed across 94 one-second frames and its full transcript. The CBS video was reviewed across 161 one-second frames and its full transcript. Neither contained a hidden source slate that established an approved demolition plan.

## 7. Source and Corroboration Problems

**Materiality: Moderate. Minute-order corroboration is overstated.**

- **Page bytes:** “the September 17 minute order as reproduced by two admitted carriers” and UPI “Checks out against the same language reproduced by the parties' counsel.”
- **Record bytes:** UPI carries the complete operative sentence, including “more than 30 days.” [UPI pin](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/data/sources/coverage/kennedy-center/article_0727.txt:11) Beatty's counsel instead quotes only “any changes to the scope of the project plans […] including but not limited to any ‘demolition’,” then paraphrases the period as “30 days.” [Counsel release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/data/sources/kennedy-center/beatty-counsel-reaction-2026-09-17.txt:3)
- **Correct account:** The lineage is one inaccessible underlying order, one full secondary reproduction, and one partial quotation from a litigant. The counsel release does not reproduce the same language and is not independent corroboration of the exact boundary.

**Materiality: Minor. The coverage denominator is inconsistent.**

- **Page bytes:** “five coverage records checked against those documents.”
- **Record bytes:** The manifest admits six coverage records: ABC, AP/PBS, UPI, two USA Today records, and CBS. [Manifest](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-sol/checks/manifests/kennedy-center--2026-09-17-30-day-notice-before-demolition.json:371)
- **Correct account:** Six coverage records were admitted, while only three receive an explicit cross-source verdict. The page should state the correct denominator and explain how each admitted coverage record was used.

## 8. Reconstructed Story

Judge Christopher Cooper's September 17 order denied an emergency hearing but required the defendants to give the plaintiffs written notice more than 30 days before implementing changes to the project scope previously presented in the preservation case, including demolition. The order did not approve demolition, prohibit it permanently, or establish that an approved demolition plan existed.

The concern did not begin with Trump's September 16 remarks or the cropped placard. On August 24, DOJ had already told the court that denial of recognition could leave the Center to be taken down and possibly replaced by an amphitheater. Preservation plaintiffs treated that as a change from the sworn no-demolition position on which Cooper's May ruling had relied.

Separately, the board voted in August to close the main building for a two-year renovation, then ratified that action on September 15 after receiving the full Delta report. Defendants' September 18 filings supplied their expanded safety, cost, schedule, and governance case for dissolving the injunction. Those assertions were not yet adjudicated, and key consultant reports remained inaccessible.

The preservation plaintiffs publicly called for possible neutral supervision. Thousands then joined a September 18 protest involving patrons, artists, workers, and preservation advocates. Trump did not clarify whether he intended demolition. The established position as of September 19 was therefore narrower than either side's rhetoric: no accessible approved demolition plan was found, but demolition and replacement had been raised by the administration in court and remained an unresolved possibility subject to advance notice and further litigation.

## 9. Remaining Unknowns

This audit is bounded. The remaining high-value checks are:

- The authenticated, court-hosted September 17 minute order.
- The sealed JLL plan and complete Delta report, including appendices.
- The September 15 board packet, minutes, and other exhibits attached to Floca's September 18 declaration.
- Independent engineering evidence for the alleged building-wide hazard, as distinct from management summaries.
- The complete, original-resolution placard sequence and its creator or source.
- Any docket entries filed after ECF 89 through September 19.
- Whether an actual project-scope change or demolition design existed outside the accessible record.
- The September 23 status report and any extension of the temporary closure, which were not yet due.
- Subsequent rulings on dissolution, summary judgment, discovery, or preservation relief.

This read-only audit took about 18 minutes. No repository files were modified.

